WEBVTT

00:00:01.450 --> 00:00:05.240
<i>Our eyes firmly fixed on Nationals, 
we, the Mifune Dolphins, have made it</i>

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.250
<i>to the second round of Regionals.</i>

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:10.720
<i>Nijigaoka Beetles' ace, Tamaki,</i>

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.610
<i>has finally taken to the mound.</i>

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.660
According to Urabe, he's an Eephus 
pitcher who mainly throws slower balls.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.830
That's why we practiced
hitting pitches at half speed.

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:23.160
What's an Eephus again?

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:26.620
Put simply, it's an extremely slow pitch.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.750
I wonder what his throw will be like.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:30.880
Play!

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:39.320
That's mega slow!

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:42.560
Strike!

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:45.090
That's even slower than I imagined!

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:47.530
But it's just slow, nothing more.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:49.620
If he got a no-hitter with those pitches,

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.130
their opposition in the first round
must've been very weak.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:57.840
That's some relaxed pitching
 from such a big youngster.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.090
With throws like those, they'll claw
back those two runs in no time.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:05.580
No, if the balls were just slow, 
they'd be easy to hit.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.690
But with a slow rainbow pitch like that,

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:12.840
the impact point becomes tiny, 
so getting a hit is quite difficult.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:15.830
Strike two!

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:19.930
And because they use a softer ball, 
even if you do make contact,

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:24.810
it won't go far unless you hit 
the ball pretty much dead center.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:29.150
Out.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:30.990
He's good.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:34.020
That was a hard hit to field.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.860
Well, they do have Komori as their manager.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:38.480
It's not surprising
their defense is so solid.

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:16.460
The Key to Success

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:15.880
Batting second, catcher,

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:17.000
Shigeno-kun.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.010
Catcher

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.010
Shigeno Daigo

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:20.840
Mifune Dolphins

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:20.840
Coach  Fujii

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:20.840
Go Daigo!

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.430
Center Field

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.430
Satou Hikaru

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.430
Right Field

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.430
Sakura Mutsuko

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.430
Good luck, Daigo-kun!

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:25.620
Dai-chan's up!

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:26.510
Yeah.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:29.060
<i>The Eephus pitch.</i>

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:31.600
<i>Their tactic is to baffle
you with a slow ball,</i>

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.670
<i>and then use their strong
defense to pick you off.</i>

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:36.270
Nijigaoka Beetles

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:36.270
Pitcher  Tamaki

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:34.920
Play!

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:38.660
<i>Satou-san showed me how 
to deal with slow balls,</i>

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:41.400
<i>and using that technique, 
I managed to get a hit.</i>

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:47.960
<i>That pitch wasn't as slow as this one, 
but the technique is the same.</i>

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:49.910
<i>Don't be hasty.</i>

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.420
<i>Wait for it to get really close...</i>

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:56.920
<i>Then, swing at your normal spot!</i>

00:03:58.300 --> 00:03:58.990
He did it!

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:00.040
Heading to center field!

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:01.110
He got a hit.

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:04.970
Out!

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:06.250
What?

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:07.360
He caught it.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.970
Why did they even have someone there?

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.050
Nice catch!

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:14.320
What the hell?

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.240
Usually, that'd be a hit to center field.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.480
Why was he standing there?

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:20.580
Two down, two down!

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:26.950
<i>So that's how they got a no hitter. 
It wasn't just an iron-tight defense...</i>

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:28.460
Damn.

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:30.070
And that was a good one, too.

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:31.760
What a waste of a hit.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.150
They're using an infield 
shift for the Eephus.

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:34.830
Huh?

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:38.370
I don't know if they're 
using signs to co-ordinate it,

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:41.390
but they change their formation 
as soon as the pitch is thrown.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:46.970
Guess it's their way of improving the odds
 of picking us off once we hit their slow ball.

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:49.010
Work out how to counter it on the bench.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:54.570
Batting third, pitcher,

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:55.770
Urabe-kun.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:57.390
Urabe-kun!

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:59.020
Pitcher

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:59.020
Urabe Hayato

00:04:57.390 --> 00:04:59.020
Urabe-kun!

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:00.900
What?

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.780
An infield shift for the Eephus?

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:03.820
That's right.

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:06.740
Mifune Dolphins

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:06.740
Manager  Tashiro

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:07.040
Somehow, they're shifting 
positions with the pitch.

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:08.280
Really?

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:13.450
Strike!

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:17.910
<i>Three fielders between second and third?</i>

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:22.050
<i>And around first, there's 
only the first baseman!</i>

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:23.550
What's going on?

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.140
Their whole right side is empty!

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:30.140
I see. That's what they're up to.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:33.200
With a slow ball, there's no 
such thing as swinging too late,

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.560
so for a right-hander, 
the ball will usually veer left.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:38.980
So tightening up the defense 
on that side makes sense.

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:42.030
Then, all we have to do 
is hit it out to right field.

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:44.430
We can rip apart an infield 
shift like that, no sweat!

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:47.340
Easy for you to say! They're grade schoolers.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:50.540
They're having a hard enough time 
just hitting these rainbow balls...

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:53.150
You honestly think they can hit
to opposite field, too?

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:02.040
Foul!

00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:04.680
Urabe's trying to hit it to right field.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:08.300
But even with his skill, 
the ball has a mind of its own.

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:10.190
<i>Damn.</i>

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:13.470
<i>I haven't practiced this kind of hit, 
so it's not surprising I'm finding it hard.</i>

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:20.230
<i>Guess all I can do is 
smash it out of the park!</i>

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:21.560
<i>Huh?</i>

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:25.270
An uncaught third strike!

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:26.600
Run, Urabe!

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:30.530
Safe!

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:32.130
He did it!

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:34.790
Yes! We've got a runner!

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:36.260
All right!

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:39.420
Huh?

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:42.130
What are you doing, Hikaru?!

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:43.760
You're up next!

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:47.390
Oh, yeah, I'm batting fourth, aren't I?

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:49.820
I'm sorry, Tamaki!

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:51.680
Don't sweat it.

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:54.180
These short, arcing pitches 
always end up bouncing.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:57.760
They're the kind of throws 
that make catchers cry.

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:05.420
We don't have any data on their cleanup,
 so I won't go straight for him.

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:07.940
Manager told me to be wary of him, too.

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:11.160
So in this case, we'll use Plan B.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:12.280
Okay.

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:14.960
Batting fourth, center fielder,

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:16.200
Satou-kun.

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:19.790
That's the kid who made that 
great catch in center field.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:22.830
If he's playing cleanup, 
he must be a good batter, too.

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:25.410
So the Dolphins has an all-rounder, huh?

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:28.470
Huh? Didn't I tell you about him?

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:30.880
That boy is Toshi-kun's son.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:31.640
Huh?

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:36.620
That's the boy who got Daigo 
interested in baseball again.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:38.520
Seriously?

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:39.840
Play!

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:44.330
<i>An uncaught third strike is 
pretty lame, but whatever.</i>

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:46.420
<i>At least I managed to get on base.</i>

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:48.570
<i>Once I'm out here, I get to dictate the game.</i>

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:51.450
<i>Stealing bases is a cinch on slow balls!</i>

00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:54.990
He's running!

00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:56.530
Ball!

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:05.090
The hell? He just started running without 
waiting to see what would happen?

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:08.130
He could've thrown a fastball 
from the slide step!

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:12.240
No, Urabe's looking at his form
and starting his run based on it.

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:12.930
Huh?

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:17.640
With that slow pitching motion, he can wait
to confirm it and still steal the base.

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:17.640
Suzuki Andy

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:20.870
<i>What's up with this pitcher?</i>

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:23.890
<i>He's still throwing Eephus 
pitches when there are runners?</i>

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:26.610
<i>A runner can do whatever he wants!</i>

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:28.610
<i>That's a ridiculous tactic.</i>

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:32.150
<i>Slow ball!</i>

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:38.540
Wh-What?

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:39.740
So fast!

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:42.530
Out!

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:45.490
Three outs!

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:46.110
Change sides!

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:47.220
Yes! Time to switch!

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:48.750
Nice one, Catcher.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:52.700
What... was that pitch just now?

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:55.220
Nijigaoka

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:55.220
Mifune

00:08:58.610 --> 00:08:59.920
Strike! Out!

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:01.770
Three outs! Change sides!

00:09:02.030 --> 00:09:03.780
Good job, Urabe!

00:09:04.850 --> 00:09:07.320
Looks like things have 
calmed down this inning.

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:11.020
The two runs they scored off 
no hits in the first hurt bad.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:14.280
But if it's only a two-run lead, 
we've got a chance.

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:17.420
Question is, how do we defeat that guy?

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:24.040
Manager, that last pitch he threw... 
what kind of ball was that?

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:28.880
That's what they call "a snap throw."

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:34.190
It's an infielder's throw that doesn't
 require a big swing of the arm or a windup,

00:09:34.190 --> 00:09:36.010
like a pitcher's or an
 outfielder's throw does.

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:41.800
By twisting your forearm and releasing 
from your fingertips with a snap,

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:43.500
you can hasten your throwing motion.

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:50.340
Most grade schoolers don't have strong 
enough arms to execute the technique.

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:54.680
With his physique, though, 
it's not surprising he can do it.

00:09:55.810 --> 00:09:57.780
Who's batting for the Dolphins?

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:01.410
Satou, you're up.

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:06.290
But I batted last in the last inning, 
so now it's the fifth batter's turn.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:09.090
You idiot!

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:12.420
Urabe got out as a runner,
 meaning you're still at bat!

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:14.890
Huh? Oh, really?

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:16.110
Lucky!

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:18.330
Sorry about that!

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:20.530
Play!

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:29.190
Strike!

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:33.140
<i>They were already struggling 
when it was just slow balls,</i>

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:36.900
Nijigaoka Beetles

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:36.900
Manager  Komori

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:36.900
<i>but now they've seen that fastball, 
their hands are basically tied.</i>

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:45.410
Strike two!

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:50.130
This isn't good.

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:51.960
This situation's a hard one to handle.

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:57.440
That fastball isn't so fast, but when 
it comes hot on the heels of a slow ball,

00:10:57.440 --> 00:10:59.510
it seems impossibly quick.

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:03.920
What's more, when a throw like 
that comes from such a slow windup,

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:05.510
it's really hard to time your swing.

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:07.780
Just like it's hard to hit a change-up,

00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:10.460
when the pitcher's stance 
suggests a fastball's on its way.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:14.210
For a grade schooler 
to overcome an Eephus pitch

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:18.690
<i>and</i> this pitching style in just 
one or two at bats is nigh impossible.

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:29.090
That was so cool!

00:11:29.090 --> 00:11:32.250
I see you've still got your reflexes, honey!

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:33.380
You think so?

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:35.460
Maybe I should start playing again!

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:39.170
That old geezer has a good throw on him.

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:40.470
<i>But this guy...</i>

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:45.600
<i>He may have swung late,
but he managed to hit my snap throw.</i>

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:49.600
<i>I see now why our manager was wary of him.</i>

00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:51.410
<i>Don't rush it.</i>

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:52.600
<i>Slow and steady.</i>

00:11:54.020 --> 00:11:58.100
<i>His form tells me nothing about what kind 
of throw is coming until he releases the ball.</i>

00:11:58.540 --> 00:12:01.280
<i>Isn't there anything I can use to 
figure out what he's about to throw?</i>

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:06.700
Ball!

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:08.330
Yeah! Good eye!

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:12.660
M-Manager!

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:16.020
Ball.

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:17.040
Time out!

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:18.080
Satou!

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:25.680
<i>What's this?</i>

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:27.210
Got it.

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:30.680
Great observation, Daigo.

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:31.920
It's nothing.

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:37.190
Their fielders shift positions for 
the Eephus just as he releases.

00:12:37.610 --> 00:12:41.340
I figured there must be some sort of sign, 
so I've been watching them all closely.

00:12:41.720 --> 00:12:42.320
Play!

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:46.480
Of course, I wouldn't be able to tell 
what the signs themselves meant.

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:50.950
But if you look closely, you'll see that 
the fielders prepare differently.

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:55.360
When it's a slow ball and they have 
to shift, everyone stands naturally,

00:12:56.230 --> 00:13:00.610
but when a fastball's coming, 
everyone drops their center of gravity.

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:07.550
Go!

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:13.560
A center fly.

00:13:14.530 --> 00:13:15.940
Nice, Okegawa!

00:13:17.660 --> 00:13:19.770
That's their leadoff with the fast legs.

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:22.280
So their outfield is iron-tight, too...

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:27.240
Shortstop

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:27.240
Nagai

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:27.240
Kimura

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:26.520
If they can catch a ball like that, 
we won't get any hits.

00:13:27.570 --> 00:13:29.180
Don't worry about it!

00:13:29.180 --> 00:13:32.260
We can now tell what pitch is coming,
 based on the fielders' body language.

00:13:32.260 --> 00:13:33.700
This battle has just begun!

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:35.850
Batting fifth, third baseman,

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:39.840
Third baseman

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:39.840
Ariyoshi

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:37.240
Ariyoshi-kun.

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:41.560
<i>So if they're crouched 
down, it's a fastball,</i>

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:43.920
<i>and if they're not, it's a slow one.</i>

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:46.090
<i>Slow ball!</i>

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:48.090
<i>And a slow ball it is!</i>

00:13:52.590 --> 00:13:53.460
Out!

00:13:55.960 --> 00:13:58.020
Batting sixth, left fielder,

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:59.020
Left fielder

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:59.020
Kishimoto

00:13:58.020 --> 00:13:59.020
Kishimoto-kun.

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:03.230
Strike! Batter out!

00:14:03.230 --> 00:14:05.490
First baseman

00:14:03.230 --> 00:14:05.490
Matsubara

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:05.490
Strike! Batter out!

00:14:05.860 --> 00:14:07.760
Three outs! Change sides!

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:21.780
<i>Knowing what pitch was coming 
basically changed nothing.</i>

00:14:22.750 --> 00:14:27.010
<i>Other than Hikaru, nobody else 
got clean hits on either type of pitch.</i>

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:31.310
<i>On top of that, under Manager Komori,
 the Beetles have an ironclad defense.</i>

00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:35.770
Nijigaoka

00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:35.770
Mifune

00:14:31.900 --> 00:14:35.540
<i>With the score at two to nothing, 
we're heading into the final stages.</i>

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:37.080
Ball.

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:37.980
Ball four!

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:40.300
All right! Okegawa's on base!

00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:42.430
No outs, runner on first!

00:14:42.430 --> 00:14:45.560
<i>After scoring two runs in the first, 
I thought we'd rack up runs easily,</i>

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:48.470
<i>but Urabe-kun's giving it his all out there.</i>

00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:53.590
<i>But his weakness is his lack of stamina, 
and his control has started failing him.</i>

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:56.410
<i>Since our lineup's good this inning, 
we'll add some insurance runs.</i>

00:14:56.410 --> 00:14:58.460
Batting second, shortstop,

00:14:58.460 --> 00:14:59.830
Ageo-kun.

00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:02.450
<i>This guy's gonna try to steal again.</i>

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:06.300
<i>With Shigeno's arm, the throw he did in 
the first inning is the best he can offer.</i>

00:15:08.690 --> 00:15:09.800
Safe!

00:15:10.340 --> 00:15:14.800
<i>I've gotta keep trying to pick him off 
and delay his start as much as possible.</i>

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:21.940
Good job, Urabe!

00:15:21.940 --> 00:15:23.400
Make him fight for it!

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:29.440
Ball!

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:34.540
This is bad.

00:15:34.540 --> 00:15:37.280
His control was already shaky this inning,

00:15:37.280 --> 00:15:39.920
but because his focus is on the 
runners, he's hurrying his pitches.

00:15:41.310 --> 00:15:42.190
Ball!

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:47.680
Urabe, don't worry about the runner!

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:49.840
There's no sense in walking the batter!

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:51.220
Calm down and pitch freely!

00:15:56.520 --> 00:15:57.240
<i>Wait.</i>

00:16:02.910 --> 00:16:04.200
Ball three!

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:07.770
Jeez... Are you all right, Tobe-kun?

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:09.780
Pull yourself together, Urabe!

00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:12.060
<i>What should I do?</i>

00:16:12.490 --> 00:16:14.320
<i>Should I call a timeout?</i>

00:16:14.730 --> 00:16:18.940
<i>But I'm in no position to tell him 
I won't let them steal,</i>

00:16:18.940 --> 00:16:20.790
<i>or to try and calm him down.</i>

00:16:21.750 --> 00:16:22.910
Damn it.

00:16:24.700 --> 00:16:25.480
Ball!

00:16:25.480 --> 00:16:26.580
Ball four!

00:16:31.700 --> 00:16:34.680
Wasn't that a strike just now?

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:37.100
The umpire's a bit harsh, don't you think?

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:41.560
No, that was probably Daigo's fault 
more than it was the umpire's.

00:16:42.460 --> 00:16:45.980
Hey, Tashiro! This game's over 
if we give up any more runs.

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:49.730
We've got Satou as 
a fully-fledged relief pitcher.

00:16:49.730 --> 00:16:52.690
Urabe's exhausted. We should make
the switch sooner rather than later.

00:16:53.990 --> 00:16:55.140
You're right.

00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:56.480
We should make a switch.

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:59.490
But not the pitcher. The catcher.

00:17:00.610 --> 00:17:02.000
The catcher?!

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:05.550
Andy, you know I don't
really want to risk you,

00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:06.970
but this is an emergency.

00:17:06.970 --> 00:17:08.330
Sorry about this, but can you play?

00:17:09.170 --> 00:17:10.550
Sure.

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:14.430
I mean, if we lose here, getting back 
to full fitness won't matter much, anyway.

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:16.220
Time out!

00:17:26.970 --> 00:17:27.880
Huh?

00:17:27.880 --> 00:17:29.190
Daigo-kun's going off?

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:32.400
So he noticed, after all.

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:36.410
I can tell you're tired, Urabe, 
but you're not at your limit yet.

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:39.570
Daigo's the one who has reached his limit.

00:17:40.310 --> 00:17:41.100
What?

00:17:41.530 --> 00:17:42.690
What?

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:45.320
He's switching out the 
catcher, not the pitcher?

00:17:46.580 --> 00:17:47.500
Really?

00:17:47.500 --> 00:17:52.630
They're going to switch out Daigo-chan, 
when the pitcher walked two batters?

00:17:53.590 --> 00:17:56.630
Why am I getting switched out?

00:17:57.890 --> 00:18:02.600
It's been on my mind since last inning, 
but it looks like you haven't noticed yet.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:09.560
We wouldn't have walked those two batters
if you'd caught the ball properly.

00:18:09.770 --> 00:18:10.450
Huh?

00:18:11.560 --> 00:18:16.030
On the riskier pitches, you haven't been 
catching the ball with proper framing.

00:18:16.850 --> 00:18:21.740
You only used your hand, and after that, 
you let the mitt move and shake.

00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:24.760
We can't really blame the umpire 
for calling them as balls.

00:18:25.730 --> 00:18:29.610
It's your first game as catcher, and the
nerves and constant focus have you beat.

00:18:30.100 --> 00:18:34.320
A catcher who can't catch properly 
anymore must be switched out.

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:39.380
Are you swapping someone out?

00:18:39.380 --> 00:18:40.440
Yes, sorry.

00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:44.460
But I want you to replace 
Kishimoto in left field.

00:18:45.310 --> 00:18:48.460
There's no telling if Andy's leg will hold out.

00:18:49.020 --> 00:18:52.520
If I take you off the field entirely, I won't
 have anyone left who can play catcher.

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:54.500
Yes, sir!

00:18:55.330 --> 00:18:56.440
Kishimoto!

00:18:56.440 --> 00:18:57.320
Huh?

00:18:58.030 --> 00:19:01.050
<i>Naturally, as Tashiro-san 
used to play catcher himself,</i>

00:19:01.590 --> 00:19:04.860
<i>he noticed Daigo-kun was playing badly.</i>

00:19:06.290 --> 00:19:10.580
<i>He was left out of the starting lineup 
because his leg is injured.</i>

00:19:10.750 --> 00:19:16.280
<i>But if you're on the field,
 we'll push you as hard as we can.</i>

00:19:18.300 --> 00:19:22.550
Aw, man, I got swapped out
 as collateral damage.

00:19:22.550 --> 00:19:25.260
I guess I struck out twice, 
so I can't really complain.

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:27.100
What do you mean you can't complain?!

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:29.580
Why the heck are those three 
newcomers still out there,

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:33.280
when we've been benched despite 
being on the team for three years?!

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:35.270
Sorry, but don't lump me in with you.

00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:37.460
I'm in the starting lineup.

00:19:39.230 --> 00:19:41.450
So they put Daigo out into left field.

00:19:44.680 --> 00:19:46.370
<i>I didn't notice at all.</i>

00:19:47.660 --> 00:19:50.960
<i>Even though Satou-san had 
stressed how important framing was.</i>

00:19:51.170 --> 00:19:53.350
<i>Without noticing, 
because of my exhaustion,</i>

00:19:53.350 --> 00:19:55.750
<i>I reverted to lazily grabbing at
the ball without moving my body.</i>

00:19:58.010 --> 00:20:02.340
<i>Just by catching the ball wrong, 
I was holding the pitcher back...</i>

00:20:03.940 --> 00:20:04.860
Play!

00:20:05.710 --> 00:20:07.960
Is Andy's leg really okay?

00:20:07.960 --> 00:20:11.060
Will he be able to put any 
force through it if he needs to?

00:20:11.060 --> 00:20:12.980
I told him not to push himself too hard.

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:17.980
I just hope our opponents get intimidated 
by Andy's arm and stop running so freely.

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:24.990
Strike!

00:20:27.750 --> 00:20:29.110
Nice one!

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:32.540
<i>We gave away back-to-back walks.</i>

00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:35.260
<i>They must want to go easy 
now and do a sacrifice bunt.</i>

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:37.260
<i>Don't sweat it, Urabe.</i>

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:39.960
<i>Dead center's just fine. 
We're going to corner them!</i>

00:20:44.210 --> 00:20:45.550
Strike two!

00:20:45.550 --> 00:20:46.420
All right!

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:48.880
I see.

00:20:48.880 --> 00:20:50.390
So he's their regular catcher.

00:20:50.790 --> 00:20:52.730
Their synchronization's a cut above.

00:20:53.290 --> 00:20:57.310
Of course, he's also more skilled 
in the position than Daigo.

00:20:57.920 --> 00:21:00.820
If the catcher has a strong 
arm and good technique,

00:21:01.180 --> 00:21:03.570
the pitcher can throw 
without a care in the world.

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:06.640
Ball.

00:21:07.390 --> 00:21:09.090
Okay, okay!

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:11.330
But that's not all.

00:21:11.990 --> 00:21:16.490
A battery has a synchronization to it. 
You can't get that in just a couple of days.

00:21:17.610 --> 00:21:19.920
Their throws to each other, 
the signs they use...

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:24.340
the considerate act of cleaning
the ball and talking to the pitcher...

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:28.940
A long-term synchronization with the catcher

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:33.850
calms the pitcher and gives him courage
in a tight spot.

00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:37.480
He's running!

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:38.520
It's a hit and run!

00:21:39.210 --> 00:21:39.640
Wha—

00:21:39.640 --> 00:21:40.690
Strike! Out!

00:21:40.690 --> 00:21:41.650
He missed!

00:21:41.650 --> 00:21:42.400
A pitchout?!

00:21:45.430 --> 00:21:46.380
Out.

00:21:49.990 --> 00:21:51.620
Nice, Andy!

00:21:52.160 --> 00:21:53.790
Yeah! A double play!

00:21:57.100 --> 00:21:57.960
Nice!

00:21:58.320 --> 00:21:59.820
<i>They got us.</i>

00:21:59.820 --> 00:22:02.880
<i>I didn't expect him to have the 
courage to waste a pitch there.</i>

00:22:04.150 --> 00:22:05.380
<i>Wow!</i>

00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:09.140
<i>So that's a real catcher?</i>

00:22:09.850 --> 00:22:11.640
Two outs!

00:22:12.470 --> 00:22:15.020
Hmph, not bad.

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:20.970
Batting fourth, pitcher,

00:22:20.970 --> 00:22:22.290
Tamaki-kun.

00:24:01.280 --> 00:24:03.320
The game has reached the closing stages!

00:24:03.580 --> 00:24:05.460
What? Sakura made an error?

00:24:05.780 --> 00:24:08.630
No, it was a trap our
 opponents set for us.

00:24:09.420 --> 00:24:11.920
Curse them for playing dirty!

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:16.820
We're going to pull together as a team 
and turn this game around, no matter what!

00:24:17.250 --> 00:24:19.290
This is a no-holds-barred match now!

00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:25.020
All-Out Battle

00:24:20.030 --> 00:24:22.430
Next time: "All-Out Battle!"

00:24:22.430 --> 00:24:24.520
Run to the stage of your dreams!
